SQL Server VM Review Guide

Overview

This guide describes practical checks and good practices for SQL Server virtual machines hosted on VMware. It focuses on settings that are worth reviewing because they can affect performance, stability, and supportability. These points are especially useful during troubleshooting and performance reviews, but they are also good to keep in mind when building or validating a SQL Server VM.
1. Check whether the VM looks oversized

One of the first things to check during review is whether the VM simply became too large over time. This often happens when CPU or RAM was added during an earlier incident and nobody later checked whether the extra resources were still justified. VMware recommends right-sizing virtual machines and keeping them within as few NUMA nodes as practical, because that usually improves CPU-to-memory locality and reduces latency.  

Typical finding
A SQL Server VM originally had 16 vCPUs. At some point performance complaints appeared, so the VM was increased to 24 or 32 vCPUs. Months later, nobody remembers why the increase was made, and the VM is still large even though CPU may not have been the real bottleneck. VMware explicitly recommends right-sizing instead of carrying over physical-server habits of overprovisioning.  

What to check
Look at the current number of vCPUs and the amount of RAM and ask a simple question: does this still look intentional, or did the VM just grow over time?

2. Check CPU layout, not only CPU count

Do not look only at the total number of vCPUs. Also check how those vCPUs are split into sockets and cores. VMware says that, as a rule of thumb, the cores-per-socket ratio should reflect the hardware configuration, while also taking care to expose a sensible NUMA topology.  

Typical finding
The ESXi host shows:

* 1 physical socket
* 32 cores per socket

The SQL Server VM has 24 vCPUs, but it is configured as:

* 12 sockets x 2 cores

This usually does not look like a sensible layout for SQL Server. It creates an artificial socket structure inside the guest that does not resemble the host. In most cases, fewer sockets with more cores is the better direction, provided the final layout still makes sense for the host.  

Why it matters
SQL Server is sensitive to CPU and memory layout more than a simple application server. VMware’s guidance says VMs should fit within as few NUMA nodes as possible, and Microsoft says SQL Server automatically creates soft-NUMA when it detects more than eight physical cores per socket.  

Where to check in vCenter
Host → Configure → Hardware → Overview
VM → Edit Settings → CPU

3. Do not assume that more CPU will fix performance

A common troubleshooting mistake is to assume that a slow system simply needs more CPU. VMware warns that over-allocating vCPUs can add overhead, and its SQL Server guidance recommends keeping VM sizing practical instead of assuming bigger always means faster. VMware also recommends, for initial high-performance sizing, that the total vCPUs assigned across VMs not exceed the total number of physical cores on the host.  

Typical finding
A SQL Server VM has a high vCPU count already, but users still report inconsistent response times. The VM is increased again, yet performance does not clearly improve. This is often a sign that the real issue was elsewhere, or that the larger VM is now less efficient.  

What to check
If a VM was enlarged in the past, verify whether that change was based on evidence such as real CPU pressure, not just on a general feeling that “the server was slow.”

4. Review CPU and memory together

During troubleshooting, do not review CPU in isolation. VMware’s SQL Server guide explicitly discusses memory-wide VMs and explains that memory size can affect whether the overall VM layout is efficient, even when the vCPU count alone looks acceptable. The guide also shows that automatic vNUMA can create an unbalanced topology for memory-wide VMs.  

Typical finding
The CPU count does not look extreme, so at first glance the VM appears fine. But the VM also has a very large amount of RAM assigned. The final result may still be a VM layout that is less efficient than expected for SQL Server. VMware’s figures and discussion on memory-wide VMs highlight exactly this kind of case.  

What to remember
Do not ask only, “How many vCPUs does this VM have?”
Also ask, “Does the combination of CPU and memory still make sense?”

5. Check CPU Hot Add carefully

The fact that CPU Hot Add is enabled is not automatically the main problem. The more important question is whether CPU was actually changed while the VM was running. VMware says that, for older Windows and vSphere combinations, customers should not enable CPU hot plug as a general practice, especially for VMs that benefit from vNUMA, and that right-sizing is a better choice than relying on CPU hot plug. VMware also says this should be a case-by-case decision and should not simply be enabled in a SQL Server VM template.  

Typical finding
A SQL Server VM inherits CPU Hot Add from a generic template. That alone may not cause an immediate issue. But if CPU was later increased online and used as a normal resizing method, that is much more relevant during review than the checkbox itself.  

Team guideline
For SQL Server VMs, CPU changes should normally be planned and applied while the VM is powered off. CPU Hot Add should not be treated as the standard way of resizing a production SQL Server VM. This is a practical guideline based on VMware’s recommendation to prefer right-sizing over CPU hot plug and to avoid using CPU hot plug as a general practice.  

What to check
If CPU Hot Add is enabled, confirm whether it is only present as a setting or whether CPU was actually resized online.

6. Review RAM sizing and SQL Server memory settings

SQL Server will use memory aggressively, so RAM settings should always be reviewed on purpose. Microsoft says that before setting max server memory, you should review the overall memory needs of the server and leave room for Windows and memory allocations outside SQL Server’s direct memory controls. Microsoft also provides a practical starting approach: first reserve memory for the OS, then subtract additional memory for other allocations outside max server memory, and use what remains as the SQL Server setting.  

Typical finding
A SQL Server VM is given a large amount of RAM because “SQL Server will use it anyway.” Later nobody checks whether the workload actually needs that much memory, and max server memory was never reviewed. The VM may still run, but the configuration is no longer intentional.  

Team guideline
Do not treat large RAM allocation as automatically good. During review, check both the amount of RAM assigned to the VM and the SQL Server memory settings inside the guest. Microsoft explicitly recommends reviewing max server memory rather than leaving it unchecked.  

What to check

* Is the amount of RAM assigned to the VM based on real need?
* Has max server memory been reviewed?
* Does the OS still have enough headroom?  

7. Do not leave max server memory without review

A very common review finding is that the VM has plenty of RAM, but SQL Server memory limits were never checked after deployment. Microsoft says max server memory should be used to account for the needs of the operating system and other applications or components on the same host. The settings take effect without a server restart, so this is something that can be reviewed and corrected without treating it like a rebuild task.  

Typical finding
The VM has 64 GB or 128 GB of RAM, but SQL Server max memory is still at a default or previously unreviewed value. Over time SQL Server consumes memory as expected, but Windows has less headroom than the team assumed.  

What to check
Inside SQL Server, confirm that max server memory reflects a deliberate choice and not a forgotten default.

8. Treat min server memory as optional, but deliberate

min server memory does not need to be used everywhere, but Microsoft says it can be important in virtualized environments because it helps reduce the risk that host memory pressure causes SQL Server to deallocate memory from the guest beyond what is acceptable for performance. Microsoft also says SQL Server is not guaranteed to allocate the min amount at startup.  

Typical finding
A team configured min server memory, but only because they assumed it should always be close to max server memory. That is not the intended reason to use it. Microsoft’s guidance describes min as a control for memory retention under load, not a simple mirror of max.  

Team guideline
If min server memory is used, it should be configured deliberately and only after understanding why it is needed in that VM.  

9. Check VMware Tools

VMware says it is critical that customers regularly upgrade or update VMware Tools for production VMs. The guide notes that VMware Tools delivers bug fixes, stability fixes, security fixes, and other enhancements for devices such as VMXNET3 and PVSCSI. It also says VMware strongly encourages incorporating VMware Tools servicing into standard lifecycle management.  

Typical finding
The SQL Server VM looks stable and there is no obvious outage, but VMware Tools is several versions behind. That may not explain every performance issue, but it is still a review finding worth planning for maintenance.  

Where to check in vCenter
VM Summary → VMware Tools status

10. Check VM compatibility / hardware version

VMware says VM compatibility determines the virtual hardware available to the VM, including CPU and memory characteristics, and recommends upgrading compatibility when new physical hardware is introduced to the environment. VMware also says this should be planned carefully, with backup and VMware Tools review as part of the process.  

Typical finding
The SQL Server VM was built years ago, the platform was later upgraded, but the VM hardware version was never revisited. The VM still runs, so the issue stays invisible until someone performs a broader review.  

What to check
Verify whether the VM compatibility level still matches the current platform standard.

11. Check the SCSI controller type

For SQL Server disks, the controller type should be reviewed rather than ignored. VMware’s guide calls out the VMware Paravirtualized SCSI Controller, PVSCSI, and notes that queue depth can be tuned for maximum performance. Even if deeper queue tuning is outside the scope of a simple review, the controller type itself is still worth checking.  

Typical finding
A production SQL Server VM has separate disks for OS, data, and log files, but the SQL disks are not attached using the controller type the team normally expects for database workloads. Even if the VM is working, this is still a useful review point.  

Team guideline
SQL Server data, log, and TempDB disks should normally use PVSCSI unless there is a clear reason not to. VMware’s guide discusses PVSCSI specifically in the context of improved I/O capability and queue depth options.  

Where to check in vCenter
VM → Edit Settings → SCSI controller

12. Check whether disks are Thin or Thick provisioned

Disk provisioning type should also be reviewed. For SQL Server, this is less about chasing perfection and more about making sure the choice was understood. VMware’s SQL Server guide includes storage best practices and discusses provisioning behavior and performance characteristics in the storage sections of the paper.  

Typical finding
A production SQL Server VM uses thin-provisioned database disks. That does not automatically mean the VM is wrong, but it should not be ignored either. During review, this should be identified and discussed, especially if the VM hosts an important workload.  

Team guideline
As a practical review expectation, SQL database disks should usually be reviewed more favorably when they are Thick Provision Lazy Zeroed or Eager Zeroed Thick rather than Thin. This is a practical interpretation of VMware’s storage guidance rather than a direct quote from a single line in the paper.  

Where to check in vCenter
VM → Edit Settings → Hard disk details

13. Quick review checklist

When a SQL Server VM is under review, check these points:

* Does the VM look oversized compared with its real workload?  
* Does the CPU layout make sense, or is it split into too many small sockets?  
* Does the VM still look sensible when CPU and RAM are considered together?  
* If CPU Hot Add is enabled, was it actually used, and were CPU changes normally made offline?  
* Is RAM sized intentionally, and has max server memory been reviewed?  
* If min server memory is used, was it configured for a reason?  
* Are VMware Tools current?  
* Is the VM compatibility level still current?  
* Are SQL disks attached using the expected SCSI controller type?  
* Are database disks thin or thick provisioned?  

Closing note

The main purpose of this guide is simple: when a SQL Server VM is being reviewed, do not treat it like a generic Windows VM. Focus on the settings that are most likely to matter in practice: sensible sizing, sensible CPU layout, deliberate SQL Server memory configuration, current VMware components, and storage settings appropriate for database workloads. That is the most useful starting point for a simple health check, troubleshooting session, or performance review.  

